INT. RADIO HUT - FLOODED RICE PADDY - NIGHT

Bamboo walls on stilts above black water. A kerosene lamp. A field radio on a crate, its dials faintly glowing. Rain drums the thatch and drips through in three places, into three cooking pots.

MAJOR WHITCOMBE (50s, British liaison, damp mustache, an unlit cigar) sits cross-legged on a rolled blanket, a map across his knees. At the radio, a SOLDIER (20s, barefoot, helmet at his feet, thin as the bamboo) twists the dial through static. In the corner, ODETTE LAMBERT (30s, field nurse, hair cut short as a boy's) wraps bandage around a wounded forearm that is, evidently, her own.

SOLDIER
Nothing. Only the ocean.

WHITCOMBE
That is not the ocean, son. That is the sound of the Pacific not talking to us.

SOLDIER
Yes, sir.

WHITCOMBE
What is your name?

SOLDIER
Tran, sir.

WHITCOMBE
First or last?

TRAN
Last. My first is Minh.

WHITCOMBE
Minh Tran. Good. You will be on the radio until dawn, Minh. If anyone answers, you wake me. If no one answers, you wake me anyway.

TRAN
Why, sir?

WHITCOMBE
Because the night is long, and I should like to be told when it ends.

Odette finishes her bandage and bites the end of the tape. She tests the knot. It holds.

ODETTE
Major, you might tell him the real reason.

WHITCOMBE
Which is?

ODETTE
That we've been cut off for three days, and you want to hear a human voice.

WHITCOMBE
Madame Lambert, you are insubordinate.

ODETTE
I'm a nurse. It's a symptom.

WHITCOMBE
Of what?

ODETTE
Of watching men pretend.

A drip lands in a pot with a clear, small note. Tran turns the dial. The static rises, falls, rises.

TRAN
Sir, may I speak?

WHITCOMBE
You may.

TRAN
My brother is in the north. He's with them.

WHITCOMBE
With them.

TRAN
With the other side. I don't know where. If I hear his voice on this radio I don't know what I'll do.

WHITCOMBE
Then you will do what a soldier does. You will wake me.

TRAN
And then?

WHITCOMBE
Then we shall see.

ODETTE
(gently)
He means he won't shoot, Minh.

WHITCOMBE
I did not say that.

ODETTE
You didn't have to.

The Major lifts the cigar to his mouth, finds it unlit, lowers it. The lamp flickers. Tran adjusts one last dial, and for a heartbeat the static thins, and a thread of faint music comes through, a woman singing in a language none of them speaks.

The three of them listen.

WHITCOMBE
Whatever that is, do not touch it.

TRAN
No, sir.

The song fades. The rain keeps falling, in three pots, in the dark.
